{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-3052",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We first write $32{y}^{3}$ as the product $16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(16{y}^{2}).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{32{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}\\cdot 2y} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ |4y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ |4|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot y\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4y\\sqrt{2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
